ORGO.reg({"types":{},"items":{"exam-q4a-1":{"id":"exam-q4a-1","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(1)","subs":"1u:NH2,2d:OCH3","given":"chairB","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:NH2,2d:OCH3|B]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Axial bonds are vertical. Equatorial bonds point outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away. Here {{NH2}} and {{OCH3}} are both equatorial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Each ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. At a carbon whose axial bond points up, the equatorial bond is the down one, and the reverse. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring, so on two neighbouring carbons the equatorial bonds are one up and one down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group up, one down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. This is the classic trap: two equatorial groups on neighbouring carbons both seem to point sideways, but they are trans."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"Both groups are equatorial, so there is no axial group to pay for. The ring flip would make both axial. This chair is the most stable one."}],"answer":"trans; yes, this is the most stable chair (both groups equatorial).","rule":"1,2: equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans."}},"exam-q4a-2":{"id":"exam-q4a-2","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(2)","subs":"1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"OH is equatorial. The isopropyl group is on a vertical bond: axial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"The axial isopropyl points down. At the neighbouring carbon the axial bond points up, so its equatorial bond, the one that holds OH, is the down one. Both groups are down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Same face: <b>cis</b>. A cis-1,2 pair always has one axial and one equatorial group."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"No. The larger group should be equatorial, and here the larger group, isopropyl, is axial. In the lecture's table an axial isopropyl costs 2.61 kcal/mol, the largest value after tert-butyl. An OH is much smaller."},{"t":"Draw the better chair","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group axial. Up and down do not change, so it is still cis.\n[[chair:1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The more stable chair is the flipped one, with isopropyl equatorial and OH axial.","rule":"cis-1,2: one axial, one equatorial. Put the larger group equatorial."}},"exam-q4a-3":{"id":"exam-q4a-3","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(3)","subs":"1u:I,4u:CH2CH3","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:I,4u:CH2CH3|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Iodine is axial (vertical bond). The ethyl group is equatorial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"The axial iodine points up. Carbons 1 and 4 are across the ring from each other, and their axial bonds point in opposite directions: at C4 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond that holds the ethyl group is the up one. Both groups are up."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Same face: <b>cis</b>. A cis-1,4 pair, like a cis-1,2 pair, always has one axial and one equatorial group."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"One group has to be axial, so the question is which. Ethyl costs 1.79 kcal/mol when axial (lecture table). Iodine is a single atom on a long C-I bond and costs much less (tables give about 0.5). The larger group, ethyl, is already equatorial: this is the most stable chair."}],"answer":"cis; yes, this is the most stable chair (the larger group, ethyl, is equatorial).","rule":"A halogen is a small axial group. A carbon group wants the equatorial position more."}},"exam-q4a-4":{"id":"exam-q4a-4","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(4)","subs":"1d:NH2,2u:Br","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1d:NH2,2u:Br|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"{{NH2}} and Br are both equatorial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"On neighbouring carbons the axial bonds point in opposite directions, so the equatorial bonds do too. One equatorial group is up and the other is down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"Both groups are equatorial. The flipped chair would have both axial. This is the most stable chair."}],"answer":"trans; yes, this is the most stable chair (both groups equatorial).","rule":"Two equatorial groups on neighbouring carbons are trans, and that chair is the stable one."}},"exam-q4a-5":{"id":"exam-q4a-5","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(5)","subs":"1u:COOH,2u:Br","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:COOH,2u:Br|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"COOH is axial (vertical bond). Br is equatorial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"The axial COOH points up. At the neighbouring carbon the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond that holds Br is the up one. Both groups are up."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Same face: <b>cis</b>. One axial, one equatorial, as always for cis-1,2."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"No. COOH is the larger group: a carbon with two oxygens on it, against a single bromine atom on a long C-Br bond (tables give about 1.4 kcal/mol for an axial COOH and about 0.5 for an axial Br). The larger group should be equatorial."},{"t":"Draw the better chair","h":"Flip the ring: COOH becomes equatorial and Br axial. Both are still up, still cis.\n[[chair:1u:COOH,2u:Br|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The more stable chair is the flipped one, with COOH equatorial and Br axial.","rule":"When one group must be axial, let it be the smaller one. Halogens are small."}}},"mols":{}});
